                                    <h2><span class="shaft"><img id="1550-0" src="//cdn.recordinghacks.com/images/icons/patterns/icon_c.png" width=27 height=27 alt="Cardioid" title="Cardioid" ></span>AKG Acoustics D 12</h2>
                    
                    <h2 class="sub">                    Cardioid Dynamic Microphone</h2>
                    <p>The D 12 is a Cardioid dynamic microphone. The D12, and its successor the D12E, are arguably the best-known early dynamics dedicated to kick drum and other bass instruments. The D12 is the nominal ancestor of AKG&#8217;s popular D112.</p><p>The moving-coil cartridge is coupled to a &#8220;bass chamber,&#8221; a cannister below the cartridge that was connected acoustically via a small tube. Research suggests that dozens of variations of D12 internals exist, however. Early AKG documentation shows a choice of several models:</p><ul><li><b>D 12</b> - 60&Omega; output, without transformer</li><li><b>D 12/250</b> - 250&Omega; impedance, via transformer</li><li><b>D 12/500</b> - 500&Omega;, via transformer</li><li><b>D 12/HI</b> - &#8220;high impedance&#8221; via transformer</li><li><b>D 12/50-250</b> - with 3-wire cable</li><li><b>D 12/200</b> - with 2-wire cable</li></ul><p>The mounting post contains a swivel join, facilitating positioning of the capsule head.</p><p>The D12 was rebranded by numerous other electronics corporations, including Philips and Echolette.<p>Permalink: <a href="//recordinghacks.com/microphones/AKG-Acoustics/D-12">AKG Acoustics D-12</a></p><p>The mic was released in 1952.</p>
